# The nameplate amounts whose unit carries a period. One row per layer whose supply is quoted as a
# rate (`4 GPU-hours per week`) as against a stock, `12 people`.
#
# Why the distinction is a relation and not an `IF`
#   "How much of the period is this supply available?" has no meaning against a stock: `12 people`
#   has no period, so "five days" has nothing to be five days of. Against a rate it can be answered,
#   and `notApplicable` there is a refusal dressed as a category error. The set of units that have
#   a period is therefore what decides whether a blank is honest.
#
# A denominator is not a period
#   The name of this file is the test it makes. `LIKE '% per %' OR LIKE '% por %'` finds a
#   denominator; this rule needs a period, and the corpus tells them apart: `asrt:factor` files
#   `GPU-hour per GPU`, whose denominator is a GPU. At the nameplate's grain the two agree on every
#   filing here, so a relation could carry the wrong test and the right rows until somebody files
#   the case that splits them.
#
# The two editions stay out of the SQL entirely
#   `shifts per week` and `turnos por semana` both file `pm:denominator/pm:period`, so nothing here
#   decides that `semana` is `week`. Written as an `OR` over two spellings, that judgement would
#   belong to no filing.
#
# `owns` is a two-part path, not an element name
#   That is what keeps this one row per layer. `Demand/amount` and `Nameplate/amount` are the same
#   element name, so an `owns` keyed on the name makes this relation answer for the demand too, and
#   return two rows per layer. A semi-join consumer cannot see the duplicate; a fault of that shape
#   sits here unnoticed until something joins on it. A window is a part of the nameplate's period,
#   and the demand's period is a different fact belonging to a different question.
#
# The layer that would disagree already exists
#   So this is live rather than waiting. `every-absence/delivery` quotes its demand `per day` and
#   files no nameplate amount at all, so a test keyed on the element name admits it on the strength
#   of a fact about demand. It costs nothing only because that layer's window is `unmeasured`, and
#   `window_not_applicable_on_a_rate` examines only `notApplicable` windows. One filing away from a
#   wrong verdict is not the same as safe.
-- pm:Claim/pm:denominator/pm:period, at pm:Nameplate/amount.
SELECT c.filing, c.layer, c.unit, c.denominator AS period
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/claims.sqlc)
) c
WHERE c.owns = 'pm:nameplate/pm:amount'
  AND c.denominator_kind = 'period'
